#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-

"""
webdrop [OPTIONS...] [FILE...]

Serve a phone-friendly web page on the LAN for moving files and text between a
mobile device and this machine.

Uploads land in a per-run directory under ~/Downloads (or under the current
directory when that doesn't exist), created when the first file arrives. Any
FILE given on the command line is offered to the browser for download;
directories are expanded recursively.

Text sent from the page is printed here. Each line typed into this terminal
appears on the page, and so does anything piped in, as one text. The page lists
every text sent this run, each with a copy button.

    webdrop                       # receive documents, photos, anything
    webdrop report.pdf notes.txt  # also hand those two files to the phone
    pbpaste | webdrop             # hand the clipboard to the phone
    webdrop -t                    # require a random token in the URL

The server listens on the first LAN interface (en*, eth*, wl*) that has an IPv4
address. A VPN tunnel is never chosen, whatever its address range. The URL is
printed at startup, and drawn as a QR code when qrencode is installed, since
scanning the terminal beats typing an IP on a phone keyboard.

Traffic is plain HTTP and, without -t, unauthenticated: everyone on the LAN can
read the offered files and the text, and write into the save directory. That is
the right trade at home and the wrong one on shared wifi, where -t makes the
URL unguessable and the QR code keeps it free to enter.

Python 3 standard library only.
"""

